import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WorkspaceStudy from '../../components/WorkspaceStudy.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Lab" description="A space for XOCE’s interface experiments, spatial desktop ideas and modern computing standards.">
  <main id="main" class="wrap section page-main"><header class="page-heading lab-heading"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="little-line"></span> The XOCE Lab</p><h1>There’s another<br />way to do this.</h1><p class="lead">A space to question familiar interfaces, try small ideas and see what makes computing feel more natural.</p></header>
    <section class="lab-study-section" id="workspace"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="label-pill">Interactive study</span><h2>Make some room.</h2></div><div class="section-intro"><p>What if a workspace let you keep a thought, a reference and a next step together? Move the windows and try an arrangement that makes sense to you.</p></div></div><WorkspaceStudy/><p class="image-caption">A small interaction study built for this website. It is an illustration, separate from the spatial desktop prototype.</p></section>
    <section class="section research-directions"><div><p class="eyebrow">Questions worth exploring</p><h2>More than<br />a new coat of paint.</h2><p>New technology is useful when it makes an ordinary thing better. These are directions for the project, with room to experiment and change our minds.</p></div><div class="research-list"><article><span class="eyebrow">01 / Interaction</span><h3>A desktop with a sense of space.</h3><p>Explore whether spatial relationships can make work easier to find, arrange and return to. The existing spatial desktop prototype is a starting point for this work; it is not distributed through this site yet.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">02 / Foundations</span><h3>Modern standards, practical benefits.</h3><p>Investigate standards such as JMAP where they can simplify software and improve responsiveness. This is a direction to explore, rather than a claim of a shipped XOCE mail app.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">03 / Devices</span><h3>The right interaction for the right screen.</h3><p>Carry useful context between desktop, phone and watch while respecting each device’s controls, performance and attention budget.</p></article></div></section>
    <div class="lab-endnote"><Icon name="spark" size={30}/><div><h2>Experiments should teach us something.</h2><p>We’ll document the question, the attempt, what worked and what needs another look. The everyday apps remain the place to put the useful lessons into practice.</p></div><a class="text-link" href="/docs/design/principles/">The design principles <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
